 ## Subtopics
 
-### Gaussian Grouping Strategy
-This strategy determines how nearby Gaussians are merged into non-leaf nodes. The sources indicate that non-leaf nodes approximate their children, but the specific grouping algorithm (e.g., clustering, spatial hashing) is not detailed in the provided excerpts [S1, unofficial]. It is a prerequisite for building the hierarchy tree.
+### [Adaptive Gaussian Merging and Retrieval](20180-adaptive-gaussian-merging-and-retrieval.md)
+A dynamic memory management and Level of Detail (LoD) strategy for 3D Gaussian Splatting that maintains a hierarchical structure based on primitive screen size to handle large scenes with limited GPU memory. Unlike static hierarchies, this method is view-adaptive, triggering merges and retrievals based on the current viewpoint's projection of the Gaussians. It should be chosen when VRAM exhaustion during online optimization is a concern, as it merges sub-pixel Gaussians into coarser parents and offloads them to CPU RAM while retrieving finer details when the camera zooms in. [Adaptive Gaussian Merging and Retrieval](20180-adaptive-gaussian-merging-and-retrieval.md)
+
+### GaussianForest
+A graph-based method introducing a tree-structured hierarchical representation for 3D scenes [S2]. It assigns explicit attributes (e.g., position, opacity) to leaf nodes, while implicit attributes (e.g., covariance matrix, view-dependent color) are shared across hierarchical levels [S2]. Additionally, GaussianForest employs adaptive growth and pruning, dynamically adjusting the representation to scene complexities [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time Complexity:** The BFS traversal is linear with respect to the number of nodes in the hierarchy. The cut condition evaluation is constant time per node.
-**Memory Complexity:** The hierarchy structure adds overhead for parent-child pointers and aggregated node properties, but allows for significant reduction in the number of Gaussians rendered per frame by skipping distant subtrees.
-**Performance:** Limits typical training to roughly 500,000 Gaussians per GB of GPU memory, imposing strict constraints on detail and extent without hierarchy [S1, unofficial]. With hierarchy, ultra-large scale reconstructions become feasible.
-**Strengths:** View-adaptive LoD, efficient memory usage for large scenes.
-**Limitations:** Requires hierarchical construction; performance depends on the quality of the cut condition and tree balance.
